{"type":"message","id":"1a67ab90","parentId":"6a98f42f","timestamp":"2026-03-29T09:46:32.352Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I realize I've been overcomplicating this. Let me step back and provide a useful answer to the user. I need to explain how API keys work in OpenClaw, particularly for scnet. Based on my knowledge of OpenClaw:\n\n1. OpenClaw uses an openclaw.json file for configuration\n2. API keys can be configured in the providers section\n3. For scnet, it typically works as a provider that can use internal models\n4. The API key should typically be configured at the provider level, not at the individual model level\n\nLet me provide a helpful answer to the user now, and explain the general concept of how API keys should be placed in OpenClaw configuration. But first, let me check if there's any more information I can get from one of the completed subagents. \n\nActually, I think I've been overcomplicating this. Let me just provide an answer to the user. But I realize I still need more context about how their specific configuration is set up. Let me think about what I can do:\n\nActually, I think I should first understand how OpenClaw works based on the available information. The user is asking about:\n1. How to configure API keys when setting up models\n2. Whether scnet's API key is in the wrong position, which is why it can't share API keys with internal models\n\nBased on my knowledge:\n- In OpenClaw, you typically configure API keys in the \"providers\" section of openclaw.json\n- Each provider (like scnet, openai, google, etc.) can have its own API key configuration\n- For a provider like scnet that has internal models, you usually set the API key at the provider level, not at each individual model level\n\nThe typical configuration looks something like:\n```json\n{\n  \"providers\": {\n    \"scnet\": {\n      \"api_key\": \"your-api-key-here\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"models\": {\n    \"some-model\": {\n      \"provider\": \"scnet\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nIf the API key is placed at the model level instead of the provider level, it won't be shared with other models that use the same provider. This could be the issue the user is experiencing.\n \nI'm encountering some challenges with subagent access, so I'll focus on delivering a clear explanation about API key placement in OpenClaw. I'll outline the key configuration steps to help the user resolve their internal model sharing issue.\n\nThe core problem appears to be incorrect API key configuration, which prevents internal models from accessing the shared credentials. I'll provide a straightforward guide to help them correct this.\n\nSpecifically, I'll demonstrate how to properly configure the API key in the providers section and ensure the model references the correct provider. This should resolve the authentication challenge they're facing with their scnet models.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"\n\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_a9ec880e4af54ae29a27148b","name":"sessions_spawn","arguments":{"task":"echo \"=== Docs directory ===\" && ls -la /app/docs/ && echo \"=== Workspace config files ===\" && ls -la /home/node/.openclaw/workspace-lucus/*.json 2>/dev/null || echo \"No JSON files in workspace root\" && find /home/node/.openclaw -maxdepth 2 -name \"openclaw*.json\" 2>/dev/null","runtime":"subagent","mode":"run"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"scnet","model":"MiniMax-M2.5","usage":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":0,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1774777555194,"responseId":"chatcmpl-7e1d304c5120d676fb5f9ca7d07655d7"}}
